# Lanes and names

`lib/ports.ts` is client-safe. No server imports.

A `Mode` picks one lane. Keep this mapping in `laneFor`. Do not add a second one.

| Mode | Lane |
| --- | --- |
| `car` | `passenger.standard` |
| `ready` | `passenger.ready` |
| `sentri` | `passenger.nexusSentri` |
| `walk` | `pedestrian.standard` |
| `truck` | `commercial.standard` |
| `fast` | `commercial.fast` |

`toneForMinutes` is the only mapping from a wait to a `Tone`. `readLane` uses it:

- Port status that does not match `/open/i` → `closed`
- Lane status `n/a` → not offered (`available: false`)
- `lanes closed` → `closed`
- `delayMinutes` is `null` → `pending`
- Otherwise ≤15 `clear`, ≤45 `busy`, above that `slow`

`defaultMode` is the lane a newly saved crossing leads with: the first of `car`, `walk`, `truck` whose status is not `n/a`, otherwise `car`.

`portName` turns CBP's uppercase names into `title` and `subtitle`. A few Brownsville bridges show the name people use: Veterans International is De los tomates, B&M is Viejo, and Gateway is Nuevo. Cards and toasts use `title`. City bookmarks use `city`. `paletteFor(portNumber)` is a stable hash, so a port keeps its card color.

`crossingStream(port, streams)` returns the live camera for Nuevo (Gateway) and Viejo (B&M): a playlist and the IPCamLive alias. `streams` is the shared `camera_stream` map, keyed by alias. A missing row uses the address in this file. Every other crossing returns null.
